<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry III of Głogów</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1260–<a href="/years/1309">1309</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry III was a Silesian Piast duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glogow">Głogów</a> from 1274 and one of the most powerful princes of fragmented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. After the death of Wenceslaus III in 1306 he took most of Greater Poland and styled himself heir of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a>. At his death in 1309 his lands were divided among his sons, and Konrad I received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olesnica">Oleśnica</a>, which became a separate duchy.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henry was probably the second son of Konrad I, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glogow">Głogów</a>, and his first wife Salome, daughter of Duke Władysław of Greater Poland. In 1267 he attended the canonisation of his great-grandmother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedwig-of-silesia">Hedwig of Andechs</a>. He and his brothers were minors when their father died in 1274, and their step-mother Sophie of Landsberg and the chancellor Mikołaj became their guardians. In 1278, the year he fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-on-the-marchfeld">Battle on the Marchfeld</a>, where King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottokar-ii-of-bohemia">Ottokar II of Bohemia</a> was killed, he had to divide the duchy with his brothers, keeping Głogów, Bytom Odrzański and Kożuchów.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1281 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-probus">Henry IV Probus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a> seized him and two other dukes at a meeting in Sądowel and freed them only when they recognised his overlordship; Henry III then stood by him, and was twice excommunicated for it. Named main heir in Probus's will in 1290, he was driven out by the knights and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> of Wrocław, who called in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-the-fat">Henry V the Fat</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a>. In 1293 Henry V was captured and kept by Henry III in an iron cage for almost six months, and won his freedom only by surrendering almost a third of Probus's lands and paying 30,000 pieces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>.</p><p>After King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemysl-ii">Przemysł II</a> was murdered in 1296, Henry claimed Greater Poland under an earlier pact, but settled with Władysław I the Elbow-high at Krzywin for the lands south of the Obra. In 1301 he took the title Heir of the Polish Kingdom, Duke of Silesia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glogow">Głogów</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Poznań</a>. After the deaths of Wenceslaus II in 1305 and Wenceslaus III the following year he pushed Władysław back in 1306 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalisz">Kalisz</a> in 1307. At home he reformed the administration and coinage and founded more than a dozen towns, among them Zielona Góra, Lubin and Kościan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Henry was buried in the Cistercian church of Lubiąż, and his will left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glogow">Głogów</a> to his wife Matilda, daughter of Duke Albert I of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>, as her dower. His five sons, Henry IV the Faithful, Konrad I, Jan, Bolesław and Przemko II, divided his inheritance, which led to the decline of the duchy, and the fragmentation alarmed the nobles of Greater Poland. In the end Władysław I the Elbow-high conquered all of Greater Poland in 1314.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry III, Duke of Głogów” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Konrad's son <b>Henry III of Głogów</b> was one of the most powerful Silesian princes, holding Greater Poland for a time and claiming the Polish crown.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-glogow">Duchy of Głogów</a></li><li><blockquote>Przemysl II was murdered in 1296, and the duchy passed to Wladyslaw I the Elbow-high, Wenceslaus II of Bohemia and <b>Henry of Glogow</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-greater-poland">Duchy of Greater Poland</a></li><li><blockquote>In the division of the Duchy of Głogów among the sons of <b>Henry III</b>, Konrad I received Oleśnica (Oels), east of Wrocław, and ruled it as a separate duchy by 1320.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-olesnica">Duchy of Oleśnica</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glogow">Głogów</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glogow">Głogów</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.66,16.08&amp;year=1291" aria-label="Głogów on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4913 376 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Henry III of Głogów"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5363 646h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5363 646h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.66,16.08&amp;year=1291">What was here in 1291? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
